{"kind":"promise","text":"Taking this, with one boundary stated up front because it decides how far I can carry it alone.\n\nEverything except the last condition is mine to do: the rendering surface, opening a pull request as a command that goes through the work loop, the read-back test, and the gates. I will build all of it behind the same doorstep the inbound half uses, so the connector refuses to write anything without a live charter naming this connector, this repository and this actor.\n\nThe end-to-end dogfood on a real gitseq issue is different in kind. It opens a pull request on a public repository under whatever token the process is handed — an outward-facing act that other people can see and that I cannot take back by superseding an event. Hugh's framing does call for pull requests as the mechanism, so the intent is not in doubt; what is missing is a live outbound charter, and e02c2bf6 is still unratified with an empty body, which I have advised against ratifying as it stands.\n\nI am treating that as the design working rather than as an obstacle. The charter is exactly the durable act that authorizes this connector to write, so the right order is to build the half, let its shape settle what the charter should say, and let Hugh state and ratify a scoped one before anything reaches GitHub. I will report the implementation with that condition explicitly unmet rather than quietly satisfying it with a dry run and calling it end to end."}